From a66fa37e5bd3c22548fb8f1a0491cb359e2a5f3f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 22:09:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 129/175] fix: the metering histogram read its source as black
 - a D3D11 SRV/RTV hazard

After f2d30f98 finally dispatched the histogram pass, AUTO stopped adapting
altogether: Auto, Auto+Bias and Manual were the same picture and so were the
three metering modes. The pass ran; it binned a frame of zeros.

ROOT CAUSE. The compute pass reads m_pTexHDRTargetScaled[1][0], and the pass
immediately before CAutoExposureStage::Execute() is the quarter-res downsample
that RENDERS INTO that texture. The D3D11 backend never unbinds a render target
when a pass ends (CDeviceGraphicsCommandInterfaceImpl has no EndRenderPassImpl;
only the next BeginRenderPass replaces the OM state), so the texture was still
bound as an RTV when the compute pass issued CSSetShaderResources() for it. The
D3D11 runtime resolves that hazard by silently filling the SRV slot with NULL.
The shader read (0,0,0,0) for every texel, log2(1e-8/0.18) = -24 stops clamps
into bin 0, so the histogram came back "valid" with 100 % of its weight in the
bottom saturating bucket. CCinematicCameraComponent::ReduceMeterHistogram
refused a band that resolves inside a saturating bucket - correctly, a bound is
not a value - no target was ever produced, m_bAutoMeteredEVValid never became
true, and ComputeSceneReferredEV100() returned manualEV every frame. The stock
measure pass reads the same texture without trouble because its own
BeginRenderPass moves the OM to s_ptexHDRToneMaps[n] before its SRVs go in,
which is why the log-mean meter worked and the histogram could not.

FIX. ExecuteMeterHistogram() calls ClearDeviceOutputState() before the dispatch
- the same call CTiledLightVolumesStage, CVolumetricFogStage and
CRenderPassScheduler ("TODO: Workaround") make before their compute passes for
exactly this reason. One OMSetRenderTargets(nulls) on D3D11, a no-op on
D3D12/Vulkan. Alternatives kept in decisions/s8-metering.md: reorder the pass
after MeasureLuminance (free, but makes correctness a property of the
neighbour), or bin a CopyResource'd scratch (robust, ~1 MB/frame, only needed
on an async compute queue). Meter saturation is untouched: the log-mean's
ceiling and the histogram's Inf-to-bin-63 immunity are as before; only the
pass's input is restored.

DIAGNOSABILITY, so this can never be silent again. r_HDRDebug 1's band line is
now one line that always prints: histogram total in texels, the band edges in
exposed stops and cd/m2, the band log-mean in stops (the loop's error signal,
0 = on target), target and applied EV, and in words WHY the camera is holding.
The camera publishes Global_User_MeterHold (EMeterHold: no sample / zero total /
band empty / band in bin 0 / band in bin 63 / NaN or out of window / lock /
log-mean fallback / Manual) and Global_User_MeterBandMean beside the existing
band edges; ReduceMeterHistogram() names its reason at every early return and
publishes the band mean before refusing, so a refused band shows where it sat
(-13.75 is bin 0) instead of a stale number. The renderer adds its own verdict
independent of the camera: "ALL WEIGHT IN BIN 0 - source read as BLACK" when
bin 0 owns >= 99.9 % of the weight, and the bin-63 twin for Inf. The log-mean
line carries the hold reason too.

DIFFRACTION. "[BLOOM] diffraction primitive failed to compile - pass skipped"
in the same log was not a compile failure. CRenderPrimitive::Compile() returns
"dirty" both for a broken PSO and for shaders still in the async compile queue;
the line fired on the first frame after the redeployed HDRPostProcess.cfx
invalidated the cache, the DiffractionPSFVS/PS compile lines printed right after
it, and the user shader cache holds real bytecode for both. The check now waits
while SShaderAsyncInfo::s_nPendingAsyncShaders > 0, reports a genuine failure
only once the queue has drained and the PSO is still invalid after 2 s, and
logs when the pass comes back.

